A shed has a rectangular cross-section topped by a semicircle, as shown. The rectangular part is $10\text{ m}$ wide and $4\text{ m}$ high. The shed has a uniform cross-section and is $9\text{ m}$ long. Find its volume to $1$ decimal place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(10,0)--(10,4) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=5]--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,4)--(10,4);
\path (5,0) node[below] {$10\text{ m}$};
\path (0,2) node[left] {$4\text{ m}$};
\path (5,10) node[above] {Cross-section};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A rectangular metal block measures $14\text{ cm}$ by $9\text{ cm}$ by $5\text{ cm}$. Two separate cylindrical holes, each of diameter $4\text{ cm}$, pass straight through the $5\text{ cm}$ thickness. Find the volume of metal remaining to $1$ decimal place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (14,9);
\draw (4,4.5) circle[radius=2];
\draw (10,4.5) circle[radius=2];
\draw (2,4.5)--(6,4.5);
\path (4,4.5) node[above] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\path (7,0) node[below] {$14\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,4.5) node[left] {$9\text{ cm}$};
\path (7,10) node[above] {Front face; equal holes};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A hollow pipe has outside radius $7\text{ cm}$, inside radius $5\text{ cm}$ and length $16\text{ cm}$. Find the volume of pipe material to $1$ decimal place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) circle[radius=7];
\draw (0,0) circle[radius=5];
\draw (0,0)--(7,0);
\draw (0,0)--(0,-5);
\path (3.5,0) node[above] {$7\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,-2.5) node[left] {$5\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,8) node[above] {Cross-section};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A tunnel is $14\text{ m}$ long. Its opening is a semicircle of diameter $8\text{ m}$. The concrete shell is $0.5\text{ m}$ thick, giving an outside radius of $4.5\text{ m}$. Find the volume of concrete to $3$ decimal places.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (-4.5,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=4.5]--(4,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=4]--cycle;
\draw (-4,-0.7)--(4,-0.7);
\draw (-4,-0.5)--(-4,-0.9);
\draw (4,-0.5)--(4,-0.9);
\path (0,-0.7) node[below] {$8\text{ m}$};
\path (0,5) node[above] {Cross-section};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A tank has a lower rectangular chamber measuring $3\text{ m}$ by $2\text{ m}$ by $0.5\text{ m}$ and an upper cylindrical chamber of radius $0.8\text{ m}$ and height $1.5\text{ m}$. The chambers are connected, their interiors do not overlap, and both fill completely. Ignore wall thickness. Using $1\text{ m}^{3}=1000\text{ L}$, find:
(a) the total capacity in litres, to $1$ decimal place;
(b) the time to fill the empty tank at $150\text{ L}$ per minute, to $1$ decimal place.
